        <span class="fm-art-eyebrow">Buying Guide</span>
        <h1 class="fm-art-title">How to Choose the Perfect Rug Size for Every Room</h1>
        <div class="fm-art-meta-bar">
          <span>The Farsh Mahal Team</span>
          <span class="fm-art-meta-dot"></span>
          <span class="fm-art-meta-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2" ry="2"></rect><line x1="16" y1="2" x2="16" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="2" x2="8" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="10" x2="21" y2="10"></line></svg>
            <span>January 12, 2026</span>
          </span>
          <span class="fm-art-meta-dot"></span>
          <span>6 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="fm-art-body-sec">
      <article class="fm-art-body-inner">
        <p>The right rug doesn't merely fill a space — it defines it. Choosing the correct size is one of the most important design decisions you'll make for a room, and one that too often gets overlooked. Below, our design team walks you through the guidelines we use with clients across the country.</p>

        <h2>Living rooms</h2>
        <p>In the living room, aim for a rug large enough to sit under the front legs of all seating pieces at a minimum. For a more grounded, generous feel, choose a rug that fits all four legs of every piece — this is our preferred approach in most American homes.</p>

        <blockquote class="fm-art-quote">
          <p>“A rug that's too small floats. A rug that's the right size grounds the entire room and makes it feel considered.”</p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Dining rooms</h2>
        <p>Under a dining table, allow at least 24 inches of rug to extend past each edge. This ensures the back legs of chairs remain on the rug even when pulled out — a small detail that changes how the room feels every day.</p>

        <h2>Bedrooms</h2>
        <p>For a king or queen bed, we love the softness of a 9 × 12 or 10 × 14 rug centered under the bed, with 2–3 feet extending on either side. That first warm step in the morning is worth the investment.</p>

        <h2>Halls and entryways</h2>
        <p>Runners work beautifully in hallways longer than six feet, leaving 4–6 inches of floor visible on each side. Round rugs are unexpectedly wonderful in circular foyers or beneath round tables — they soften the geometry of a room instantly.</p>
        <p>When in doubt, size up. A slightly larger rug will almost always feel more considered than one that sits awkwardly small in the middle of the room.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

