        <span class="fm-art-eyebrow">Craft</span>
        <h1 class="fm-art-title">Understanding Hand-Tufted Rugs</h1>
        <div class="fm-art-meta-bar">
          <span>Priya Sharma</span>
          <span class="fm-art-meta-dot"></span>
          <span class="fm-art-meta-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="18" rx="2" ry="2"></rect><line x1="16" y1="2" x2="16" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="2" x2="8" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="10" x2="21" y2="10"></line></svg>
            <span>December 28, 2025</span>
          </span>
          <span class="fm-art-meta-dot"></span>
          <span>8 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="fm-art-body-sec">
      <article class="fm-art-body-inner">
        <p>Hand-tufting is one of the most versatile, characterful, and painterly rug-making techniques in the world. Unlike hand-knotting — which is beautiful but rigidly geometric — hand-tufting allows artisans to work with brush-like freedom, sculpting depth, color and form with remarkable precision.</p>

        <h2>How a Farsh Mahal rug is made</h2>
        <p>Each rug begins with a full-scale drawing on cotton canvas stretched across a wooden frame. Skilled artisans use a hand-held tufting gun to punch wool yarn through the canvas, following the design line by line, color by color.</p>
        <p>Once the tufting is complete, a layer of latex is applied to the back to hold the tufts in place. A cotton backing is then affixed by hand, and the pile is sheared, carved, and finished — sometimes in high-low sculpted textures, sometimes flat and even.</p>

        <blockquote class="fm-art-quote">
          <p>“Between 120 and 220 hours of hand craftsmanship go into each Farsh Mahal rug.”</p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>Why it matters</h2>
        <p>Hand-tufting gives rugs a richness and depth that machine-made pieces can't replicate. It also allows for a much broader range of design — from painterly abstract landscapes to crisp modernist grids.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

